# ==============================================================================
# StockAssist AI — Backend Docker build-context exclusions (PH2.1)
#
# This file is the SINGLE AUDITABLE BOUNDARY between the repository and the
# container image. The Dockerfile uses `COPY . .` deliberately: rather than
# maintaining a per-file allowlist inside the Dockerfile (which silently breaks
# whenever a new module is added), everything that must NEVER reach the image is
# denied here, in one reviewable place.
#
# Two independent things are achieved by every line below:
#
#   1. SECURITY — anything excluded here cannot be baked into an image layer.
#      Deleting a file in a later layer does NOT remove it: every layer is
#      retained and readable by anyone who pulls the image. The only reliable
#      way to keep a secret out of an image is to keep it out of the build
#      context in the first place.
#
#   2. CACHE + SIZE — the build context is tarred and shipped to the Docker
#      daemon on every build. A stray `venv/` or `node_modules/` adds hundreds
#      of megabytes of transfer and invalidates the `COPY` layer on every
#      unrelated file change, destroying layer caching.
#
# Rule of thumb: deny by default, allow deliberately. When in doubt, exclude it
# — a missing runtime file fails loudly at startup; a leaked secret fails
# silently and permanently.
# ==============================================================================

# ── SECRETS (highest priority — never relax these) ────────────────────────────
# server.py calls `load_dotenv(ROOT_DIR/'.env', override=True)`. The `override=True`
# means a `.env` present inside the image would SILENTLY WIN over the environment
# variables injected by Docker/Kubernetes/Railway at runtime. Excluding it here
# is therefore both a secret-leak control AND a correctness control: with no
# `.env` in the image, load_dotenv is a no-op and real container env vars apply.
#
# `.env.example` is committed and placeholder-only, but it is documentation, not
# runtime input — it is excluded too, so the image contains no dotenv file of
# any kind and there is no ambiguity about where configuration comes from.
.env
.env.*
*.env
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json
*credentials.json*
*token.json*
.credentials

# ── Python virtual environments ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host venvs are platform-specific (macOS/arm64 wheels here) and would be both
# useless and actively harmful inside a linux image. Dependencies are installed
# from requirements.txt in the builder stage instead.
venv/
.venv/
env/
ENV/

# ── Python caches & compiled artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────
# Bytecode compiled by a different interpreter/platform is invalid in the image;
# the Dockerfile precompiles fresh bytecode with the image's own interpreter.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
.mypy_cache/
.pytype/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage/
htmlcov/
.tox/
.nox/

# ── Tests & dev-only tooling ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The runtime image installs requirements.txt ONLY (pytest/black/flake8/mypy live
# in requirements-dev.txt — PH1.11 finding M14). Shipping tests without their
# dependencies would produce a directory that cannot run; shipping the
# dependencies would enlarge the attack surface of a production server for no
# operational benefit. Tests run in CI (PH2.5/PH2.6), not in production.
tests/
test_*.py
*_test.py
conftest.py
pytest.ini
requirements-dev.txt

# ── Test & CI RESULT artifacts (PH3.12 finding C-1) ───────────────────────────
# The rules above exclude the tests. They do NOT exclude what RUNNING the tests
# leaves behind, and that gap put a file into a certified release image.
#
# `backend-ci.yml` runs pytest with `--junit-xml=test-results/junit.xml`, so the
# moment anyone executes the project's own test command — in CI or locally — a
# `test-results/` directory appears in the build context. It is git-ignored
# (.gitignore:69), so it is invisible to `git status` and absent from every
# commit, and `COPY . .` swept it into the image anyway. PH3.12 measured the
# result: a build from a working directory produced 117 files under /app, while
# a build from a clean `git archive` of the SAME commit produced 116. The image
# was therefore not a function of the commit — two engineers building "the same
# release" got different artifacts, and the extra one carried a developer's
# machine hostname and a record of a failing test.
#
# WHY THE PATTERNS ARE BROADER THAN THE ONE FILE THAT WAS FOUND
# The specific leak was `test-results/junit.xml`. Excluding exactly that would
# fix this instance and none of the next ones: every entry below is a path some
# common runner writes by default, and each would leak the same way. The rule
# being encoded is "test INPUTS and test OUTPUTS are both build-context
# garbage", not "this one filename".
#
# NOTE ON WHY .gitignore IS NOT ENOUGH. Docker does not read .gitignore. Any
# git-ignored artifact under backend/ enters the image unless it is ALSO named
# here — which is precisely the class of defect C-1 belongs to. When adding a
# new generated-artifact path to .gitignore, add it here too.
#
# WHY EVERY ENTRY IS WRITTEN TWICE, PLAIN AND `**/`-PREFIXED
# A .dockerignore pattern is matched against the path relative to the build
# context ROOT, so a bare `test-results/` excludes `<context>/test-results/` and
# nothing else. Verified empirically against Docker 29.4.0 rather than assumed:
# with only the bare patterns, `sub/test-results/junit.xml` and `sub/junit.xml`
# were both still copied into the image. The `**/`-prefixed form is what makes
# the rule depth-independent. Both forms are kept because `**/foo` does not
# match a root-level `foo` — dropping the bare pattern would reopen the exact
# hole this block closes.
test-results/
**/test-results/
test_reports/
**/test_reports/
junit*.xml
**/junit*.xml
*.junit.xml
**/*.junit.xml
coverage.xml
**/coverage.xml
nosetests.xml
**/nosetests.xml
report.xml
**/report.xml
.benchmarks/
**/.benchmarks/
.hypothesis/
**/.hypothesis/

# Depth-independent twins for the generated tool artifacts already listed in
# "Python caches & compiled artifacts" above. Those entries are correct but
# root-anchored, so a `services/htmlcov/` or a nested `.coverage` written by a
# per-package test run would still have been copied in. Same defect class as
# C-1; closed here, beside the reasoning, rather than doubling the length of
# the block above.
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.coverage
**/.coverage.*
**/coverage/
**/htmlcov/
**/.tox/
**/.nox/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.pytype/

# ── VCS & CI metadata ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.git` in particular can be enormous and contains the FULL history — including
# any secret ever committed and later removed from the working tree.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/
.gitconfig

# ── Editor / IDE / OS noise ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Build outputs & JS toolchain (never part of the backend image) ────────────
node_modules/
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/

# ── Logs, temp files, local databases ─────────────────────────────────────────
logs/
*.log
tmp/
temp/
dump.rdb
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db

# ── Documentation & local notes ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Docs are valuable in the repo and dead weight in a server image. Excluding
# them keeps the `COPY . .` layer stable: editing a Markdown file must not
# invalidate the application layer and force a rebuild + redeploy.
*.md
docs/

# ── Container tooling that is not needed INSIDE the container ─────────────────
# docker/entrypoint.sh and docker/healthcheck.sh ARE needed and are NOT excluded.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
